<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naval power</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naval power is a recurring factor in imperial rise, as control of sea-lanes allows states to move armies, protect trade and project force across great distances. Maritime empires built their wealth and reach on fleets and ports. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> depended on its navy to dominate the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a> policed the straits of South-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and the British, Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> empires built global networks on sea power.</p></div>
